ns.</td></tr><tr><td>8: Continuity of a Function (Intuitive Definition)</td><td>Continuity of a Function (Intuitive Definition) covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>9: Continuity at a Point (Formal Definition)</td><td>Continuity at a Point (Formal Definition) covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>10: Types of Discontinuity: Removable</td><td>Types of Discontinuity: Removable covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>11: Types of Discontinuity: Jump</td><td>Types of Discontinuity: Jump covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>12: Types of Discontinuity: Infinite</td><td>Types of Discontinuity: Infinite covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>13: Limits of Trigonometric Functions</td><td>Limits of Trigonometric Functions covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>14: Limits of Logarithmic and Exponential Functions</td><td>Limits of Logarithmic and Exponential Functions covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>15: Exercise 15.1: Limits of Algebraic Functions</td><td>Exercise 15.1: Limits of Algebraic Functions covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>16: Exercise 15.2: Limits of Trigonometric Functions</td><td>Exercise 15.2: Limits of Trigonometric Functions covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>17: Exercise 15.3: Continuity and Discontinuity</td><td>Exercise 15.3: Continuity and Discontinuity covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr></table><!-- academic-extension:start --><section class="academic-extension" aria-labelledby="academic-extension-title"><h2 id="academic-extension-title">Academic Extension: Evidence, Conditions &amp; Open Questions</h2><p><b>Topic bridge — Limits And Continuity.</b> Use this extension to connect the topic’s core material with deeper academic inquiry: state a claim, name the conditions under which it holds, and distinguish established knowledge from an open research or interpretive question.</p><h3>Established academic fact</h3><p>The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus links accumulation and rate of change: for continuous f, the derivative of ∫ₐˣ f(t)dt equals f(x). A limit describes a function’s behaviour near a point; the function value itself need not equal that limit.</p><h3>Conditions, assumptions &amp; limits</h3><p>Check continuity and the interval before applying the theorem; a correct formula still needs correct limits, units and sign. In applications, the integral accumulates a rate only when the variable and units are defined consistently.</p><h3>Open research question — The Riemann Hypothesis</h3><p>This unsolved conjecture concerns the non-trivial zeros of the zeta function and has deep connections to the distribution of prime numbers. Checking many cases computationally cannot replace a proof for all cases.</p><h3>Inquiry prompt</h3><p>For “Limits And Continuity”, identify one assumption in the model, explain what observation would test it, and state what evidence could distinguish the accepted account from the open question “The Riemann Hypothesis”.</p><h3>Further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/5-3-the-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus">OpenStax: Fundamental Theorem of Calculus</a></li><li><a href="https://www.claymath.org/millennium/riemann-hypothesis/">Clay Mathematics Institute: Riemann Hypothesis</a></li></ul><p><small>Curriculum note: the research question is an enrichment extension, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed NEB text and syllabus for examinable scope.</small></p></section><!-- academic-extension:end --></body></html>